# Claims review narrative — Charness v8.14.7

## Subject and identities

Prepared release commit: `382649040d6915f9dfd0c954162b3631f5c7ed1d`; direct parent: `acf8d6d94f6ffd39eeb5e259b820b3b7f451f160`. The subject is [`latest.md`](../release/latest.md) and [`2026-10-17-v8.14.7-notes.md`](../release/2026-10-17-v8.14.7-notes.md), audited against the prepared tree and prior public tag `v8.14.6`.

The release delta from `v8.14.6` is 136 files, 8,091 additions, and 2,249 deletions. The prepared branch is local; `v8.14.7` has no tag in this checkout.

## Re-derived claims

The release-note claims check completed with status `clean`, zero findings, and zero overclaims. It re-derived the four registered surfaces as: 0 Python sources declaring their own `--json` option, 14 top-level CLI subcommands, 19 public skills, and 8 shell gates. Their item lists and declared scan limits match the notes. This check establishes agreement with those four registered derivations; it does not establish that the derivation catalog covers every possible claim.

The read-only `current_release.py` check found no version drift: packaging, Claude plugin, Codex plugin, and Claude marketplace versions are all `8.14.7`; the Codex marketplace source path is `./plugins/charness`. This matches the four-versioned plus one presence-only surfaces recorded in `latest.md`.

The three required behavior claims are supported by the prepared source:

- `charness task run` defaults self-review to `never`; `--self-review` opts in. The implementation also records self-review as a non-approval result (`charness:1677–1683`, `scripts/task_run/task_run.py:206`, `scripts/task_run/task_run_self_review.py:34–38`).
- `task status --all-repos` reports managed runtime inventory. Its boundary is immediate sibling keys in the caller’s managed runtime tree, not whole-machine coverage (`scripts/task_run/task_run_inventory.py:326–332`, `docs/agent-task-runs.md:125–129`).
- Retained reviewer attempts require an explicit boundary mode. A missing mode is rejected during delivery-field validation, so older evidence without a provable mode fails closed (`skills/shared/scripts/reviewer_delivery_fields.py:195–199`; the readback contract is also pinned in `tests/quality_gates/test_reviewer_delivery_state_machine.py:48–52`).

The persistence-risk path is present: the task-run scanner checks changed candidate-diff lines for specific destructive statement shapes, adds `persistence-risk` to review reasons, and can convert completed or validated-partial results to `completed-needs-review` (`scripts/task_run/task_run_persistence.py:17–22, 65–116`; `scripts/task_run/task_run_state.py:366–393`). The release critique’s F1 requested this operator-facing explanation, and the notes include it.

The update instructions and target version agree with the release adapter and CLI surfaces. “No migration is required” is consistent with the documented remedy for an unprovable retained attempt: rerun the bounded review after updating.

## Pending/non-claims

`latest.md` records that the release quality command exited 0 at `post-bump, pre-commit`, while `pytest-release` remains unestablished pending final resume. It also explicitly says no pre-push quality receipt was recorded. I treat that as a limited recorded result, not a final quality receipt.

The same record says initial fresh-checkout probes passed and lists their commands. My read-only `current_release.py` invocation returned `not_established` for its own probe listing because that command does not run probes; I did not rerun them. The earlier pass remains a recorded claim, not independently reproduced here.

Push, tag, GitHub publication, public release verification, post-publish install refresh, and issue closeout remain pending or unverified as recorded. The requested-review gate is `ok` with advisory-only policy and zero configured commands; that does not substitute for the claims review.

The critique is supporting context for candidate `862a8300b1b496dfb205e8a23ea44002a0101cd3` and its earlier range. It is not evidence that the final prepared notes or release-state record had already received this claims review.

## Findings

- **Advisory — persistence wording is broader than the detector.** The notes say the guard requires review when candidate content contains `DROP`, `TRUNCATE`, or an unscoped `DELETE` (notes, line 8). The scanner is explicitly best-effort and matches particular changed diff lines: `DROP TABLE/COLUMN/INDEX/SCHEMA/DATABASE`, `TRUNCATE TABLE`, and unscoped `DELETE FROM …` statements (scanner, lines 7–8, 17–22, 65–116). Consider naming those statement shapes and the best-effort diff scope to avoid implying that any keyword occurrence is detected.
- **Advisory — patch rationale could be more precise about compatibility.** `latest.md` says the patch does not change “command compatibility” (line 90). The release adds `--all-repos` and changes self-review’s default from automatic behavior to opt-in; the notes disclose both behavior changes. The rationale is defensible if it means existing command syntax remains usable, but it could say that explicitly.

No blockers found in the audited claims.

## Verdict

**pass** — the release-note claims match the prepared tree and the required behavior statements are present and supported. The advisories above do not block the claims review. Publication remains pending as `latest.md` records; that record should remain in its prepared state until this review narrative is committed.

